<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallia Belgica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Belgica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gallia</a> Belgica was a Roman province in north-eastern Gaul, organised under Augustus from the lands of the Belgae conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Durocortorum</a>, modern Reims, and it covered parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Luxembourg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> districts were later separated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and Superior.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica.jpg" alt="Gallia Belgica" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Belgica_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>In 57 BCE Julius Caesar led the conquest of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and described the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> rivers as inhabited by the Belgae, whom he said were separated from the Celtic Gauls by language, custom and laws. This definition became the basis of the later province. Modern historians read Caesar and the archaeology as placing the core of the Belgic alliance in the far north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, among the Suessiones, Viromandui and Ambiani, who were more economically advanced than allies such as the Nervii.</p><p>Apart from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remi">Remi</a>, the Belgic tribes allied against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Caesar avoided a battle with their combined forces, reported at 288,000, and defeated them piecemeal, using cavalry to skirmish. Uprisings followed, the largest led by the Bellovaci in 52 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 22 BCE Marcus Agrippa split <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> into three regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a>, Lugdunensis and Belgica, intended to be a mix of Celtic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> as the capital, which later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> at an uncertain date. The province was divided into civitates that followed tribal boundaries, with capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a>, Cambrai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, and was governed through councils at Reims or Trier. Modern historians see the division as an administrative convenience rather than a cultural one.</p><p>Around 90 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> split off the northeast as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and the east as Germania Superior, separating the military zone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> from civilian populations. In 173 Didius Julianus, then governor, repelled an invasion by the Chauci that left large farms near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> abandoned and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> burnt. In 260 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a> founded a breakaway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a>, which Aurelian ended in 274. Around 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> divided Belgica into Belgica Prima around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgica-secunda">Belgica Secunda</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> valley prospered in the fourth century while Trier was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausonius">Ausonius</a> wrote about the river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 406 a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and destroyed large parts of the province, and in 452 a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s army at the Catalaunian fields. A Gallo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-kingdom">Roman Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> survived from 457 to 486, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> then made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgica-secunda">Belgica Secunda</a> the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a>'s Merovingian kingdom.</p><p>The name Belgica remained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> until the modern period. In the 1500s the region was split between Belgica Foederata in the north and Belgica Regia under the Habsburgian crown in the south, and the name ceased to cover the whole region only after the revolution of 1831, when the rebels took it for their new country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gallia Belgica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Celtic peoples practised their religion across much of Iron Age Europe, from Ireland and Britain through Gaul and <b>Belgica</b> to southern Germany, Bohemia and Celtiberian Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Votive coins scattered over the floor of the mithraeum at Pons Sarravi (Sarrebourg) in <b>Gallia Belgica</b> run from Gallienus to Theodosius I: when the temple was destroyed, Christians apparently regarded the coins as polluted and left them behind.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching west into <b>Belgica</b> with Gepids, Ostrogoths, Alans and other vassals, Attila captured Metz, killing most of its people and burning the town; he also took Strasbourg.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their name matches the Menapii of <b>Belgic Gaul</b>, which has led to suggestions of migration or trading links across the sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/manapii">Manapii</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome Durocortorum became capital of the province of <b>Gallia Belgica</b>, with monuments such as the Porte de Mars.</blockquote><a href="/state/remi">Remi</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar was told that within living memory their king Diviciacus had been the most powerful ruler in Gaul, holding sway over much of <b>Belgic Gaul</b> and over Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/suessiones">Suessiones</a></li></ul></section>
